    "body": "**Updated by the user after a PathPilot Hub test, 2026-11-13 15:53-15:56 (770MX sim, v2.14.4).** This replaces the request above.\n\nFour hand-written programs were run (a T2 change, a square cut in the air at Z+0.25, then a different ending in each). The sim's stored G30 was low (machine Z -1.5611).\n\n| Ending | Result |\n|---|---|\n| A: `G53 G0 Z0`, `G30` (today's post) | goes up, then **G30 slams the tool down** to the G30 Z (DRO G54 Z -0.8356, below the part top) |\n| B: `G30` only | **slams down** from Z+0.5 to the G30 Z |\n| C: `G53 G0 Z0` only | ends at the top of travel and stays there (DRO G54 Z 0.7255) |\n| D: `G53 G0 Z0`, then `G0 A0` | not wanted |\n\nThe user, in their words: \"Definitely do not want 'D'. A and B have the tool slam down at the end. C is the only right one.\"\n\n**Revised request:** end the Tormach program with `G53 G0 Z0` and no `G30` after it, and no `G0 A0` return:\n\n```\nM9\nM5\nG53 G0 Z0\nM30\n```\n\nThe retract stays straight up in machine coordinates, so the operator's stored G30 can never bring the tool back down at the end.\n\n**Scope:** the program end (`onClose`). The `G30` before each tool change (`retract()` before M6) is a separate question and stays as is unless the maintainer decides otherwise. That G30 is PathPilot's tool-change convention, and the same low-G30 risk applies there, so it is worth a follow-up look.\n\n**Done means:**\n- Every Tormach program ends `G53 G0 Z0` then `M30`, with no `G30`.\n- The goldens are regenerated, and the change is explained in the PR.\n- A pin fails if a `G30` follows the final retract.\n- The C program in the Hub kit is the reference.\n\nThe title is updated to match.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
